.Dd July 13, 2026
.Dt __OS_TUNING 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm __os_tuning_check
.Nd power/kernel-tuning advisor: cheap, read-only startup probes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In os_tuning.h
.Ft void
.Fn __os_tuning_check "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn __os_tuning_check
runs a battery of cheap, READ-ONLY probes against Linux
.Pa /proc
and
.Pa /sys
knobs known to affect a latency-sensitive, thread-per-core runtime
(PLAN.md 19.21), and emits one advisory log line
.Pf ( Dv XTC_LOG_INFO ,
via the default logger in
.In xtc_log.h )
per probe that is
.Em not
already in its recommended state.
A well-tuned host produces no output at all: this is an advisor, not
a monitor, and must never spam an operator who already did the work.
.Pp
.Fn __os_tuning_check
is called once by
.Fn xtc_app_start
(unless
.Va xtc_app_opts_t.no_tuning_check
is set); it is meaningful only on Linux and is a complete no-op on
every other platform.
It never writes to any
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys
file.
Every probe FAILS SAFE: a source file that is missing or unreadable
(not present on this kernel, hidden inside a container without
access, wrong architecture) is skipped with
.Em no
log line and
.Em no
error -- "unknown" is not treated as "a finding".
.Pp
The six probes and their recommendation thresholds:
.Bl -tag -width "sched_autogroup"
.It Sy cpufreq governor
Reads
.Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_governor .
Recommends
.Sq performance
if the active governor is anything else.
Skipped if the file does not exist (no cpufreq driver, e.g. inside
many VMs/containers).
.It Sy intel_pstate
Reads
.Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/status .
Recommends switching back to
.Sq active
only when the status is
.Sq passive :
passive mode hands control to a generic cpufreq governor and gives
up intel_pstate's HWP-aware autonomous boost management, which is
worse for the throughput/latency workloads this runtime targets.
If the status file does not exist at all -- non-Intel CPU, driver not
built into the kernel, or deliberately disabled via
.Sq intel_pstate=disable
on the kernel command line -- the probe skips silently and makes NO
recommendation.
A disabled intel_pstate is usually a deliberate choice (e.g.
cycle-accurate MSR-level frequency pinning for microbenchmarking) and
this advisor has no business second-guessing it; there is
deliberately no separate
.Pa /proc/cmdline
check for
.Sq intel_pstate=disable ,
because by the time the sysfs status file has already answered
everything actionable, a cmdline check would only ever reach the
same "nothing to say" verdict.
.It Sy transparent_hugepage
Reads
.Pa /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled ,
a line of the form
.Sq "always [madvise] never"
where the bracketed word is the active setting.
Recommends
.Sq madvise
if the active setting is
.Sq always
or
.Sq never :
both extremes risk compaction stalls or latency spikes that
.Sq madvise
avoids by only using THP where an application explicitly opted in.
.It Sy vm.swappiness
Reads
.Pa /proc/sys/vm/swappiness .
Recommends a lower value if it is
.Em above 10 :
10 is the commonly recommended ceiling for a database/latency-
sensitive server (the kernel default is 60); above that the kernel
starts reclaiming anonymous pages under mild memory pressure well
before it needs to, trading throughput for headroom this kind of
workload does not want.
Exactly 10 is already tuned and produces no line.
.It Sy kernel.sched_autogroup_enabled
Reads
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/sched_autogroup_enabled .
Recommends disabling it (0) for a latency-sensitive server workload
when it reads 1: autogrouping optimizes desktop interactive fairness
by lumping a process's threads into a scheduling group, which is not
what a server wants deciding CPU time between reactor threads.
.It Sy io_uring under seccomp
Attempts a real, minimal
.Xr io_uring_setup 2
syscall (a single-entry ring) and immediately closes the resulting
fd on success; on failure it inspects
.Va errno .
.Er EPERM
specifically means seccomp (or an LSM) is blocking the syscall, and
is reported: xtc falls back to epoll, which costs throughput/latency
under load.
Any other failure
.Pf ( Er ENOSYS
on a pre-5.1 kernel,
.Er EINVAL ,
.Er EFAULT )
means "not applicable here", not "a finding", and is skipped.
No liburing or
.In linux/io_uring.h
dependency: the probe carries a local ABI mirror of the kernel's
.Vt struct io_uring_params
(stable UAPI layout since Linux 5.1).
.El
.Pp
Every probe path may be redirected to a fixture file with the
internal test-only hooks
.Fn __xtc_os_tuning_governor_path_override ,
.Fn __xtc_os_tuning_pstate_status_path_override ,
.Fn __xtc_os_tuning_thp_path_override ,
.Fn __xtc_os_tuning_swappiness_path_override ,
and
.Fn __xtc_os_tuning_autogroup_path_override
(Linux only; declared in
.In os_tuning.h ) ,
and the io_uring probe's verdict can be forced with
.Fn __xtc_os_tuning_uring_force
(0 = real probe, 1 = force "blocked", -1 = force "usable"), so every
probe's threshold logic is unit-testable without root or a real
kernel-tuning knob.
Pass NULL to restore the real path.
Not part of the stable API.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
None; this is a diagnostic side effect (log lines), not a value-
returning probe.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
Safe to call concurrently; each probe is an independent read.
The test-only override hooks are not: they are for single-threaded
unit tests only.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* Called automatically by xtc_app_start(); to opt out: */
xtc_app_opts_t opts = XTC_APP_OPTS_DEFAULT;
opts.no_tuning_check = 1;
xtc_app_create(&opts, &app);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr io_uring_setup 2 ,
.Xr __os_cpu 3 ,
.Xr xtc_app_start 3 ,
.Xr xtc_log 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn __os_tuning_check
first appeared in xtc under PLAN.md 19.21 (M14).
